  <info><![CDATA[# WHAT IS IT?

This is a model simulating sex and sexual orientation differences in short-term mating behaviors. Findings in evolutionary psychology suggest that males have a stronger desire for short-term mating than females (Regan & Atkins, 2006). They also have lower standards for a short-term mating partner (Buss & Schmitt, 1993). This model explores how males and females (heterosexual or gay) may behave differently in short-term mating under these assumptions. A systematic report and discussion of the experiments conducted using this model can be found in the paper "An Agent-Based Model of Sex and Sexual Orientation Differences in Short-Term Mating Behaviors as a Result of Mating Preferences" (Ying et al., 2024).

&nbsp;  

# HOW IT WORKS

The model runs with 300 agents in total. Agents in this model behave under the following rules on each tick:

* Move to a random point near where they were spawned and decide on whether they would like a short-term mating (i.e., whether they have a preference on short-term mating) on this tick

* If they meet someone else, the two agents have a short-term mating if (a) they both have a preference for short-term mating on this tick, (b) they both meet each other’s standards for a short-term mating partner, (c) the pair is of different sexes in the all-heterosexual model or is of the same sex in the all-gay model

*  If an agent did not have a preference for short-term mating, or had such a preference but did not actually have short-term mating on this tick, they may decide to form a long-term relationship with another agent they meet. This happens by a probabiliy specified by an adjustable parameter (see below). The model visually shows the long-term relationship status by fixing the positions of the agents and turning their patch color into grey. The coupled agents reduce their likelihood of choosing short-term mating.

&nbsp;  

# HOW TO USE IT
## Setup and Run the Model

Press SETUP to setup the model, then press GO to start the simulation. 

Note: Changes in the following sliders and switches do not take effect until the SETUP button is pressed.

*  The NUMBER-OF-MALE slider sets the number of males in the population.

* The MOVERMENT-RANGE slider controls how far agents can go away from their spawning point; if you choose a value of 15, agents can go to anywhere on the panel.

* The LONG-TERM-LIKELIHOOD slider sets the likelihood of forming a long-term relationship when two agents meet.

* The MALE-SHORT-TERM-LIKELIHOOD/FEMALE-SHORT-TERM-LIKELIHOOD slider sets the likelihood that a male/female agent chooses to have a short-term mating on each tick.

* The MALE-STANDARD/FEMALE-STANDARD slider sets male/female agents’ standard for a short-term mating partner. (Note: All agents’ desirability values form a normal distribution with a mean of 5 and a standard deviation of 1.5. This means that around 68% of all agents have a desirability value lying within 3.5-6.5, and 95% have a value lying within 2-8.)

* The ALL-HOMOSEXUAL? switch, when set on, turns everyone into gay males and females. Agents will only have short-term matings and/or form a long-term relationship with someone of the same sex.

## Observe the Model

There are six plots by which you can observe the model:

* The “In-pool population” plot shows the relative frequency of individuals who’ve ever had short-term matings.

* The “Coupled population” plot shows the relative frequency of coupled individuals separately among males, females, and all agents.

* The “Short-term mating (all)” plot shows the mean number of short-term matings averaged over the total number of males and females separately.

* The “Short-term mating (in-pool)” plot shows the mean number of short-term matings averaged over the number of males and females who’ve ever had short-term matings separately.

* The “Sexual partner (all)” plot shows the mean number of short-term sexual partners averaged over the total number of males and females separately.

* The “Sexual partner (in-pool)” plot shows the mean number of short-term sexual partners averaged over the number of males and females who’ve ever had short-term matings separately.


## Things to Notice

The default model parameter settings assume that a) the sex ratio is balanced in the population, b) everyone is heterosexual, c) males have a higher short-term mating likelihood than females, and d) females have a higher short-term mating standard than males. Under these assumptions, several interesting things are worth noticing:

* The average number of short-term mating experiences and short-term partners is exactly the same among the full population of males and females, as shown by the ostensible single curves on the plot.

* These numbers differ, however, between the population of males and females who are in the short-term mating pool: males have visibly more short-term mating experiences and partners than females on average.

* There are more females than males in the short-term mating pool. Why may this be the case and how can this explain the above observations?


## Things to Try

After you play with the default parameter setting, you can explore the model by trying the following things:

* Adjust the MALE-SHORT-TERM-LIKELIHOOD/FEMALE-SHORT-TERM-LIKELIHOOD sliders. What happens when males have the same likelihood as females? What about when females have higher likelihood than males?

* Adjust the MALE-STANDARD/FEMALE-STANDARD sliders. What happens when males have the same standard as females? What about when females have higher standards than males?

*  Adjust the NUMBER-OF-MALE slider to simulate an unbalanced sex ratio. How do the results change and what might be an explanation? You may want to look at both the cases where males outnumber females and the reverse.

* Set the ALL-HOMOSEXUAL? switch to true. To start with, you may set all other parameters to the default settings. How do the results change and what might be an explanation?

&nbsp;  

# CREDITS AND REFERENCES

Buss, D. M., & Schmitt, D. P. (1993). Sexual strategies theory: An evolutionary perspective on human mating. *Psychological Review, 100*(2), 204–232. https://doi.org/doi:10.1037/0033-295X.100.2.204d

Regan, P. C., & Atkins, L. (2006). Sex differences and similarities in frequency and intensity of sexual desire. *Social Behavior and Personality: An International Journal, 34*(1), 95–102. https://doi.org/10.2224/sbp.2006.34.1.95

Ying, Y., Antfolk, J., & Santtila, P. (2024). An Agent-Based Model of Sex and Sexual Orientation Differences in Short-Term Mating Behaviors as a Result of Mating Preferences. The Journal of Sex Research, 61(6), 946–954. https://doi.org/10.1080/00224499.2023.2183485

&nbsp;]]></info>
